Z||000000||newfie||11-09-2001||05:11 PM||||I like the idea of turbo's but can't see paying all the money for the kits.  Anyone custom made there own.  I would think you could just get an old turbo off a Daytona or old ford.  Rebuild if need be, fab up the exhaust and intake components as well as oil lines.  Reprogram the computer PROM for the needed fuel with boost(or add bigger injectors for less computer friendly people).  Seems like you could do all that for less than $1k not like the 2-3K all these companies are asking. Also wondering if anyone has turbocharged a 3.4L TDC.  I you could get some serious HP out of that with a turbo. I guess you now know what I have in mind. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||blazin'||11-09-2001||05:36 PM||||I was wondering the same thing! My grandmother has this old Cutlass or something like that, I can't think of what it is. I was wondering if it could be modified and rebuilt and put on a Fiero||||reg||1
Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||11-09-2001||05:40 PM||||Run a few searches in Technical Discussion & General Chat (and the Archives) and you'll come up with a <B>wealth</B> of info.<P>Just make sure you know what you're doing before you start!<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Borgio||11-10-2001||02:32 AM||||There is a guy named Dennis LaGrua who has a website about this. He did a custom turbo on his Fiero for under $900. He also put a 3.4 in one and turbo charged that, which is what I plan to do. His site is very informative. It hasn't seemed to be working lately though.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jake||||reg||7
Z||000004||RedOktober||11-10-2001||03:09 PM||||just one question? How cheap do you want GOOD, RELIABLE, DRIVEABLE HorsePower? unfortunately power comes at a cost. A good new turbo will set you back at least $750,Mine cost me that much to just rebuild. <P>A muffler shop can make a crossover pipe for a few hundred, but there is more to it than just a turbo. <P>There's fuel, engine management, and the simple fact that 2.8's have a bad crank, and thats from the factory!<P>by the time you piece everything you need properly, you will only save about 1000 at most from buying a kit. <BR>You really have a few choices, Quality, Price, or Performance. Choose any of the two.<P> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/red6[1]a.jpg">   <BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"<p>[This message has been edited by RedOktober (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||koolgtz||11-10-2001||05:53 PM||||If you order the back issue of HCI (Hot compacts and imports) months August and Sept 2001 you'll see the two part story about using salvage yard parts to build a powerful turbo for about $600.00. <BR>they used a turbo from a T-bird, the intercooler from an XR4TI and some custom made pipes etc.Good story||||reg||6
Z||000006||sluppy123||11-10-2001||10:19 PM||||I've done it,(did it all from scratch) so yes it can be done. Is it easy to make it all work right? not really. <P>$750 to rebuild a turbo? Damn that must be one hell of a rebuilt turbo. I pulled mine off a t-bird for $20, bought a rebuild kit for $70. So for under $100 I had a good reliable turbo<P><P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>                <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=54316496&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
